      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate troponin I >99th percentile of normal as a criterion for myocardial injury after percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Troponin I and creatine kinase monobasic (CK-MB) were measured in 327 patients before and after percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty (PTCA) with stent implantation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Troponin I was elevated before PCI in 100 of a total of 222 patients with acute coronary syndrome (ACS). In 91 of these 100 patients, troponin I was elevated also after PCI but actual increases in troponin I concentrations from before to after PCI were found in only 32 patients. The increase of troponin I correlated with post-procedural CK-MB whereas post-procedural troponin I levels did not correlate. In the 122 patients with ACS but normal/normalized troponin I before PCI and in 105 patients with stable coronary artery disease post-procedural troponin I appeared to be a reliable indicator of myocardial infarction (MI), however more sensitive than CK-MB."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Troponin I after PCI is sensitive to pre-procedural concentrations. To avoid false positive MI diagnoses we thus suggest that troponin I should be measured before as well as after the procedures and only actual increases should be regarded as indicating procedure-related MI."
        }
      ]},
